3. Composition and counterfactual interpretation

Words, singing, sound imitation, prosody and silence are different modalities, not one identical mechanism. This engine performs a deliberately simple mapping; it doesn’t recover a unique objective melody from arbitrary text. A future reader may replace the toy word-to-MIDI encoding with grapheme-to-phoneme, phonological stress, live speech timing, or learned performance systems while preserving source/provenance boundaries.

Example:

Old MacDonald had a farm, E-I-E-I… [E] {cat}!

Performance: melody (words), temporal rest (comma/ellipsis), explicitly chosen elliptical silence, sound-effect glide ({cat}), emphatic rest (!). The child might supply an expected syllable or invent a new verse. Those are different outcomes; only observation and comparisons could establish a catalytic effect.

Use the same phrase with two lenses:

U(parent, reader, child, musician) = different decoders of the same score
R(Old MacDonald) = predictable completion if shared song is known
G(Old MacDonald + open prompt) = invitation to novel continuation
Z? = possibility of a genuinely independent creation after an opening

